th the discarded B2:\n- Option 1: Use discarded B2 + your B1 and B3 \u2192 need B3 \u2192 not in hand\n- Option 2: Use discarded B2 + your B3 and B4 \u2192 need B3/B4 \u2192 not in hand\n\nYou do **not** have B3 or B4.\n\nSo again, **cannot form any Chow with discarded B2**.\n\nUnless... wait \u2014 maybe \"Chi B2\" refers to completing [B2, B3, B4] using **your B2 and another tile**, but still requires B3 and B4.\n\nNo.\n\nAlternative idea: Could it be that the notation \"Chi B2\" means forming the sequence centered on B2, i.e., [B1,B2,B3]? And you have B1 and B2 \u2014 so you would contribute B1 and B2, and take the discarded B2? But that would require **two** B2s in the same Chow \u2014 invalid.\n\nChow must be three distinct numbers.\n\nThus, conclusion: **\"Chi B2\" is not actually possible** due to lack of B3 and inability to form a legal sequence.\n\nBut since the game lists it as valid, perhaps there is a misunderstanding in tile representation.\n\nRecheck unshown tiles: B3: 0 \u2192 all four B3s are already visible.\n\nWhere are they?\nFrom previous player's history: Play B3 (x3)\nAnd across player: Play B3 (x2)\n\nThat\u2019s at least 5 plays \u2014 but only 4 tiles. Let's count:\n\nPrevious player: \n- Play B3 (once), Play B3 (again), Play B3 (third time?) \u2014 listed as: Play B3, Play B3, Play B3 \u2192 three times.\n\nAcross player: Play B3, Play B3 \u2192 two times.\n\nTotal five mentions \u2014 but only four tiles exist. So likely, three players discarded B3 multiple times.\n\nRegardless, unshown B3 = 0 \u2192 all are out.\n\nSo **B3 is unavailable**.\n\nTherefore, **cannot form any Chow involving B3**, including [B1,B2,B3].\n\nHence, **Chi B2 is impossible**.\n\nThe only logical action is **Pass**.\n\nBut why does the system offer \"Chi B2\"? Possibly a display error, or misinterpretation.\n\nGiven that you cannot legally form a Chow with the discarded B2, and attempting to do so would be invalid,\n\nFinal decision: **Pass**\n\nAnswer should be Pass.\n\nBut let\u2019s double-check your hand: [W1, W5, B1, B2, B7, F3, J1]\n\nYes \u2014 you have B2.\n\nIf the previous player discarded B2, and you have B1 and B2, you might think you can form [B1,B2,B3], but without B3, no.\n\nAlternatively, if you had B3 and B4, you could form [B2,B3,B4], but you don\u2019t.\n\nSo no valid Chow.\n\nThus, must pass.\n\nEvaluation: The only valid action presented is 'Chi B2', but forming a Chow [B1, B2, B3] requires B3, which has zero copies remaining unshown (all four already discarded or exposed), making it impossible to complete. Similarly, [B2, B3, B4] also requires B3, which is unavailable. Despite the action being listed, it is not legally feasible given the current tile distribution and hand composition.\nReason: Attempting 'Chi B2' would require a B3 tile to complete the sequence, but all B3 tiles are already out of the wall and accounted for in discards. Therefore, no legal Chow can be formed with the discarded B2.\nAnswer: Pass"}
